Title: THE CHRONICLES OF THE GUARDIANS
Episode: 101, "THE FIRST SIGNAL"
Credit: Draft 1 SHORT — PROPOSED, not approved
Author: Prepared for Ω SYD OMEGA 91717 STUDIOS GLOBAL under the founder's direction
Draft date: 2026-11-07
Notes: Cold open and three condensed acts, about 16 script pages of a 50-minute episode. It follows the Season 1 bible (continuity locks included) and is not a final draft. One deliberate correction to the bible: the beat sheet says two niches are filled in Malachar's sanctum, but Continuity Lock 1 says he holds only Time until episode 6, so ONE niche is filled here (registry conflict C-21). Frequency motif: 9.1717 Hz. Nothing here is filmed, released or playable.

FADE IN:

INT. THE GUARDIAN CITADEL — LOWER HALL — NIGHT

A hall cut from one piece of pale stone, so old that the floor is worn into shallow bowls where people once stood to wait. Lamps burn without flame, low and blue. Nothing else moves.

SUPER: "FOUR THOUSAND YEARS AGO."

ITHREN, fifties, a Guardian in a plain travelling coat, kneels at the centre of the floor. She is tired in the way of someone who has been right too many times. In her hands, a vessel the size of a closed fist, clay, sealed with wax and a thread of silver.

She presses a thumb to one flagstone. It slides aside without sound, showing a hollow lined with fine sand.

She sets the vessel in the hollow. She does not let go at once.

ITHREN
(to the empty hall)
It will not open for one hand. I made sure of that. It is the only cruelty I allow myself.

The floor around her begins, very faintly, to hum. A single low note, steady, from beneath the stone. It is not loud. It rises in the sand until the sand itself trembles into a pattern: nine rings, drawn and gone.

ITHREN (CONT'D)
For whoever holds the other end.

She lets go. The stone slides shut. The hum holds for one breath longer than it should, as if it had heard her, and fades.

SMASH CUT TO:

MAIN TITLES — NINE CONCENTRIC RINGS DRAW THEMSELVES IN LIGHT: SEVEN INSCRIBED WITH SIGNS, TWO LEFT EMPTY. THE NOTE UNDER THEM IS 9.1717 Hz.

ACT ONE

INT. MUSEUM OF PORT VESSA — RESTORATION LAB — DAY

A working lab, not a gallery: steel benches, daylight lamps, the smell of solvent. Rain on a skylight. A wall of drawers labelled by century.

AUREN VALE, 23, sits on a stool in a grey apron with a loupe on his forehead, cleaning a tray of corroded coins with a swab. He works the way some people pray: without noticing he is doing it. He is gentle with everything and has never been sure why.

NELL, 28, a conservator who carries three mugs and drinks from none, drops a folder on his bench.

NELL
Your seven o'clock is a hoard from the estuary. The curator says "careful". She means "faster".

AUREN
It's a hoard. It's been underwater since before the curator.

NELL
That's what I told her. She heard "slower".

Auren smiles, and then stops. He tilts his head, the way you do when a fridge motor changes pitch.

AUREN
Do you hear that?

NELL
The rain?

AUREN
Under the rain.

She listens, honestly. Shakes her head. On the bench the coins lie still. Then, one by one, the nearest coin shivers on its tray. A hair's width. Then the next.

AUREN (CONT'D)
(very quiet)
Don't move.

The tray hums. A thin note, so low it is felt in the teeth. Nell steps back with her mugs and the nearest one rattles against its saucer in time.

INSERT — A COIN. In its worn face, a ring pattern surfaces under the corrosion, nine circles, seven filled, two blank. Then the corrosion closes over it again.

NELL
Okay. That's not the rain.

She reaches for the lab's old display case on the far wall, the one the museum never replaced, an oak-and-brass cabinet with two keyholes set a metre apart, so that no single person can open it. Policy. Nell has one key on a lanyard. Auren has the other, on a chain at his neck.

NELL (CONT'D)
Come on. If it's the coins, I want them in the case, and I want the case shut.

AUREN
(standing, drawn)
It's not the coins.

Together they cross the room. Each puts a key in a lock. They turn them at the same moment, as the rules require, and the brass door swings out.

And the room STUTTERS.

For the length of a blink, everything pauses. The rain on the skylight hangs mid-fall. Nell's hair, lifting from the door's breeze, stops. A mug tips, stills, hangs over the floor in a long thin arc of tea.

Only Auren moves. He feels it in his chest: a thick, sweet pressure, like a held note. He looks at his own hands on the key.

It ends. Rain falls. The mug finishes tipping and breaks on the floor. Nell gasps, steps back, looks at the mug, then at Auren.

NELL (CONT'D)
(slowly)
Did you just... Did the room just... skip?

AUREN
(too fast)
It's the storm. Pressure in the pipes.

NELL
Auren.

He takes his key out of the lock as if it were hot. She holds his eyes a second too long. Then she bends and picks up the broken mug, because that is who she is, and says nothing.

NELL (CONT'D)
(gently)
I didn't see anything. If that's what you need me to say.

AUREN
(grateful, afraid)
That's what I need.

INT. UNIVERSITY OF HALDEN — ARCHAEOLOGY DEPARTMENT — OFFICE — DAY

A cramped office, books in stacks that are architecture. DR LYRA THORNE, 37, in a field jacket she has not taken off since the email, stands at a window holding a printout like a bill.

HOLLIS REEVE, 50s, her excavation director, sits on the radiator in muddy boots. TUAN PHAM, 30, a geophysicist with a camera permanently around his neck, films nothing and everything.

HOLLIS
It says "regrettably".

LYRA
It says "effective immediately".

TUAN
It says "the Foundation's priorities have shifted". Which is what they say when they've lost interest in the dead.

LYRA
(reading)
"Thank you for twelve years of contribution to the field."

HOLLIS
That's a eulogy.

LYRA
It's a eulogy for a dig that's not finished.

She turns to a wall of photographs: a slab at a ridge, covered in lines. A lattice, nodes joined by thin grooves. Her handwriting in the margin: SAME AS THE OTHERS.

LYRA (CONT'D)
Anvar Ridge isn't a one-off. The lattice repeats. Different continents, different centuries, the same pattern. Somebody was drawing a map and nobody has ever asked what it's a map of.

TUAN
We asked. The Foundation said no.

Lyra's phone buzzes on the desk. An unknown number. She looks at it, then at Hollis, then answers on speaker, thinking it will be the Foundation apologising.

VOICE (V.O.)
(a courteous man, mid-forties, unhurried)
Dr Thorne. I apologise for calling at a bad hour. My name is T. Draven. I advise a private donor who has read your Anvar Ridge paper twice and would like your work to continue.

LYRA
Which donor?

VOICE (V.O.)
One who prefers not to be a name. The renewal is security-backed, which is a boring way of saying the money will not vanish when the next board has a different mood. There is one condition: I would like to see the site.

Hollis mouths: "NO." Tuan raises his camera, almost laughing. Lyra stares at the phone as if it had spoken Latin.

LYRA
Mr Draven, I'll need to know who you work for.

VOICE (V.O.)
Of course. I'll send a letter. I think you'll find it dull. Please call me Torin.

The line goes dead. Silence.

HOLLIS
That is the most suspicious man alive.

LYRA
(already writing)
That is also twelve more weeks of excavation.

TUAN
You're going to say yes.

LYRA
I'm going to say "send the letter".

ACT TWO

INT. AN ABBEY SANCTUM — NIGHT

A vaulted room under a derelict abbey, restored with more love than money. Candles instead of lights. Along the wall, a single long shelf of dark wood with seven niches cut into it, each the size of a fist, each lined with felt.

One niche holds a stone. It is not impressive: a dull amber lump in the dark, the colour of old resin. But the candlelight bends around it, very slightly, and the flames closest lean as if listening. This is the Time stone. Six niches are empty.

MALACHAR sits at a plain table beneath the shelf. In his hands, a square of paper. He is folding a bird, slowly, with the patient fingers of an engineer. He looks calm. He has looked calm for a very long time.

At the doorway stands VESPER LUND, 30s, an exact woman in a dark coat, and behind her CORVIN HALE, 40, who fills the frame without trying and has never once been unkind in it.

VESPER
(holding a tablet)
I have a lead on the fifth stone. A private collector in the north. Anselm Crowe.

MALACHAR
(not looking up)
Crowe collects what other people cannot name. That is rarer than collecting what they can.

VESPER
He is also well guarded.

CORVIN
(mild)
I like well guarded.

MALACHAR
(smiling a little)
You like anything with a door.

He finishes the bird. It sits on his palm, small and crisp, slightly crooked at one wing. He looks at it a moment longer than a man looks at paper.

MALACHAR (CONT'D)
My daughter folded these badly. I never corrected her.

Vesper and Corvin say nothing. They have heard this once before, and have learned that silence is the only kind answer. Malachar sets the bird on the shelf, beside the one filled niche, and the bird's shadow falls across six empty ones.

MALACHAR (CONT'D)
Open the file on Crowe. I will read it in the morning.

VESPER
(a beat; not quite a question)
And the other thing? The signal.

Malachar goes still.

MALACHAR
You heard it too.

VESPER
Everyone in the sanctum heard it. For about six seconds, at four this afternoon.

MALACHAR
(very quietly)
Then someone has begun.

He looks up at the one amber stone, which, for the first time in a long while, is warm to look at.

CUT TO:

EXT. THE AURELITH — MORNING (FLASHBACK, WORDLESS)

A white city on a cliff, pale terraces running down to a green sea. A festival morning. Paper lanterns in a hundred hands.

On a ridge-top road, a small girl, six, LIORA, runs ahead of her father, a paper bird held high over her head like a kite. Malachar, younger, laughs and calls after her. We do not hear him. We hear only a single low note, rising.

She turns, grinning, and looks straight into the camera.

SMASH CUT TO:

INT. MUSEUM OF PORT VESSA — LAB — NIGHT

Auren, alone, standing at the oak cabinet with the broken mug swept away. He has been staring at his key for an hour. He puts it down. Picks it up. Puts it down again, as though it might do something if he stopped looking.

He tries a small experiment: he holds a coin in his palm and concentrates. Nothing. He says, aloud and embarrassed:

AUREN
Stop.

Nothing. He laughs at himself. He is half-turned to go when the coin on the tray gives, very gently, a single note.

He freezes. He turns back.

AUREN (CONT'D)
(a whisper)
Who are you?

The note does not answer. But on the tray, a second coin, then a third, take it up, and the three hum together in a chord, and Auren, hand to his chest, understands that the sound he has heard all his life under the world is not coming from the world.

INT. UNIVERSITY OF HALDEN — LYRA'S OFFICE — NIGHT

Lyra alone with a fresh letter on the desk: heavy paper, a plain letterhead, DRAVEN RISK ADVISORY. She has read it four times. It is dull, exactly as promised. It also contains, in the last paragraph, a sentence she cannot stop reading:

"The donor asks me to say that the pattern repeats because it is not finished."

She turns to the photograph on her wall. The lattice. Nine nodes, a ring. Seven filled, two blank.

She takes a red pen and, for the first time, draws a circle around the blanks.

ACT THREE

INT. MUSEUM OF PORT VESSA — LAB — MORNING

Rain has stopped. Nell is at her bench pretending not to watch Auren, who is pretending to work. A new mug sits by his elbow. He has not asked for it.

NELL
(not looking up)
Drink it. It's not tea. It's an apology for the mug.

AUREN
You don't owe the mug.

NELL
I owe you for not asking.

He drinks. It is the first warm thing that has gone into him in two days. Across the lab, the oak cabinet stands shut, its two keyholes dark.

INT. AUREN'S FLAT — NIGHT

A small flat above a chandler's, full of restored things nobody asked him to keep: a clock, a lamp, a cracked mirror made whole. On the highest shelf, out of habit and out of fear, a battered child's biscuit tin: his mother's, kept fifteen years, never opened. On its lid, scratched with a pin, a ring, a second ring inside, seven small marks.

He has taken it down. He sits at the window with it on his knees. He does not lift the lid. Rain again. The harbour lights smear.

He falls asleep in the chair. The room hums.

The tin box is warm.

SLOWLY, ON THE GLASS BESIDE HIM: condensation gathers where he has breathed against the window. A finger, no one's finger, draws in it. One digit at a time.

9.

1.

7.

1.

7.

Auren's eyes open. He is no longer in the chair. He is standing at the window, his own hand pressed to the glass, and he does not remember moving. The numbers are drawn at the height of his fingers.

Outside, a long way off, under the rain and under the harbour and under the whole sleeping city, a single note holds, clear as a bell.

AUREN
(barely audible)
What do you want from me?

The glass fogs over. The numbers dissolve, one by one, until only the last, the 7, is left, and then it too runs down the pane like a tear.

TAG

INT. THE GUARDIAN CITADEL — LOWER HALL — NIGHT (LATER)

The same hall, four thousand years on. Dust where there was none. The blue lamps burn, fewer than before.

SERAPHINE, ageless in a bookseller's cardigan, stands in the middle of the floor, one hand on a flagstone, as she has stood a thousand times. She has her eyes closed.

She is listening to the note.

SERAPHINE
(softly, to the empty room)
So it's begun.

She does not look surprised. She looks like a woman who has been awake a very long time and has just been told, at last, that the night is ending.

CUT TO BLACK.

CLOSING SEQUENCE — THE NINE RINGS FADE. A SINGLE LOW TONE, 9.1717 Hz, HOLDS UNDER THE CREDITS.

THE END OF EPISODE 101 (DRAFT 1 SHORT)
